WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.498
Suppose a registration assistant delivers one impressive demonstration.

2
00:00:04.698 --> 00:00:10.039
The supervisor then rates every part of the job highly without checking records or teamwork.

3
00:00:10.239 --> 00:00:14.553
That is a halo effect: one characteristic influences other ratings.

4
00:00:14.753 --> 00:00:21.158
If only last week's mistake determines the annual review, recency has displaced evidence from the full period.

5
00:00:21.358 --> 00:00:29.471
Leniency and strictness shift ratings too generously or harshly; central tendency compresses meaningful differences toward the middle.

6
00:00:29.671 --> 00:00:32.701
Personal preferences can also distort judgments.

7
00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:38.174
The visual separates an observed event from the evidence needed for each performance dimension.

8
00:00:38.374 --> 00:00:47.307
Useful safeguards include clear job-related standards, notes collected across the period, evaluator training, and a review of how standards are applied.

9
00:00:47.507 --> 00:00:52.750
Discuss disagreements about evidence instead of simply averaging incompatible opinions.

10
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:57.628
Employees should have a meaningful opportunity to explain relevant circumstances.

11
00:00:57.828 --> 00:01:05.197
A dissatisfied employee is not proof that the rating is wrong, and a completed form is not proof that the process was fair.

12
00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:08.017
Examine both the judgment and its basis.
